What is Webpack?
Definition
Webpack is a JavaScript Module Bundler. It takes all the files of a React application (JavaScript, CSS, Images, Fonts, etc.), processes them, and combines them into one or more optimized bundle files.

Webpack is a tool that bundles multiple project files into optimized files for the browser.

Why do we use Webpack?
Combines files into one bundle
Optimizes performance
Converts modern JavaScript to browser-supported JavaScript
Handles CSS and Images
Supports Hot Reloading
Reduces file size

Module Bundling
Definition
Module Bundling means combining multiple modules/files into one or more bundle files.

Example Files
Header.jsx
Footer.jsx
Home.jsx
App.jsx

Types of Modules
JavaScript Module
App.jsx

CSS Module
style.css

Image Module
logo.png

Font Module
Roboto.ttf

JSON Module
data.json


Entry Configuration
Definition
Entry tells Webpack where the application starts.

Usually
src/index.js

Syntax
entry: "./src/index.js"
React (Vite)

Example
module.exports = {
  entry: "./src/main.jsx",
};

Output Configuration
Definition
Output tells Webpack where to save the generated bundle.

Syntax
output: {
 filename: "bundle.js",
 path: path.resolve(__dirname, "dist")
}

Example
dist
   bundle.js
Entry                Output
Entry	             Output
Starting point	   Ending point
Reads files	       Creates files
Input	           Output Bundle